  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 102 PM Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Typical late-summer weather is expected, with \nmostly isolated late-day showers and storms for the next several \ndays.\n\nNear-normal deep-layer moisture, proximity to deep sub-tropical \nridge in the western Atlantic, and minimal mesoscale forcing \nmechanisms, should keep coverage of showers/storms to isolated to \nlocally scattered over the next several days. Coverage of \nshowers/storms will be locally greatest each day focused around weak \nsurface pressure trough over the Piedmont, the sea breeze as well as \nwhere moderate low-lvl instability develops, which varies by day.\n\nMost showers/storms that are able to develop through the afternoon \nand evening will remain relatively shallow and consist of mostly \nshowers with isolated/embedded storms with infrequent lightning. \nDowndrafts will be equally typical for summer-time storms and \nproduce 20-30 mph wind gusts immediately surrounding the locations \nwhere they collapse.\n\nIsolated and infrequent strong to locally severe storms remain \npossible given the moderate to strong instability in place within \nthe hot/humid air mass over the Carolinas (MLCAPE of 2000-3500 \nJ/kg). Clustering storms that collapse along a common cold pool will \nhave the best chance to produce 40-60 mph wind gusts given the lack \nof deep-layer shear to otherwise organize storms (0-6 km shear < 20 \nkts). \n\nTues through Thurs: The southern flank of a belt of enhanced west-\nnorthwest mid-lvl flow over the Great Lakes and northern Mid-\nAtlantic will slowly shift south and into the Carolinas as early as \nTues and persist over the region into the late week. During this \ntime, a slight increase to the overall synoptic deep-layer shear is \nexpected (15-30 kts, greatest towards the VA border). Additionally, \nany convectively induced perturbations and remnant MCV's from more \nvigorous convection from the OH and TN Valley's may provide locally \nmore organized and greater coverage of convection along their track \nduring this time. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Risk for hot temperatures and dangerous heat \nincrease later this week.\n\nSub-tropical ridging over the western Atlantic will continue to \nelongate while retrograding westward across FL and into the \nnortheast Gulf through Tues. Troughing pushing into the PacNW and \nwestern CONUS will help shift the persistent mid-lvl anticyclone \nfrom the Four Corners Region and build the ridge over the lower/mid \nMS Valley midweek before shifting into the TN Valley and Southeast \nlate week into the weekend.\n\nIn the absence of convection, building sub-tropical ridge to our \nsouth and west as well as warming H850 temperatures to around 20C \nwould support low-mid 90s today, and as high as upper 90s possible \nTues through Fri. Heat index values in the upr 90s to near 100 over \nthe nw Piedmont to around 105 elsewhere, are expected through at \nleast Thu. The approach and possible passage of a frontal zone will \nprobably yield less hot temperatures by Fri-Sat.",
